@startuml vibration-window
' How 160 samples become one number, and why the mean is never computed on
' its own. Grounded in Src/vibration.c (vibration_push, scaled_variance,
' integer_square_root) and in the window arithmetic in Inc/vibration.h.

title One vibration figure, from 160 samples

skinparam shadowing false

start

:sample arrives (x, y, z in counts);

:sum_x += x, sum_y += y, sum_z += z
sum_of_squares += x*x + y*y + z*z
samples++;
note right
  Two running sums and a count.
  Nothing is stored per sample, so
  the window costs 28 bytes instead
  of 160 times six.
end note

if (samples < 160?) then (yes)
  :return 0, no result yet;
  stop
else (no)
endif

:scaled = 160 * sum_of_squares
          - (sum_x^2 + sum_y^2 + sum_z^2);
note right
  This IS the variance times 160^2,
  with gravity and the part's offset
  gone, because they are the mean.

  The mean is never computed on its
  own on purpose. Gravity puts about
  256 counts on one axis and its mean
  is not a whole number, so rounding
  it would push up to half a count of
  error into EVERY sample before
  squaring. The vibration can itself
  be a count or two, so that error
  would not be rounding, it would be
  the answer.
end note

:root = integer square root of scaled;

:milli_g = root * mg_per_count / 160;
note right
  Scaled BEFORE the division. Divide
  first and everything below one
  count is lost, and one count is
  4 mg on this part.
end note

:start the next window, return 1;

stop

legend bottom
  160 samples at 800 Hz is 200 ms, so the five results a second that the
  telemetry wants fall out of the window length and no timer is needed.
  Variance adds across perpendicular axes, so shaking equally on three
  of them reads as the square root of three times one, not three times.
endlegend

@enduml
